---
import { OPERATOR } from '../config/legal';
import { LINKS } from '../config/site';
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
---

<Legal
  title="Deleting your data"
  heading="How to delete your data"
  description="How to delete your Permaily account and data, what is removed and when, what stays and for how long, and what to do if you can’t sign in."
  intro="You can delete your account yourself, at any time. This page says how, what happens at once, what is removed later, and what stays."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Delete your account</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Sign in at <a class="link" href="https://app.permaily.com">app.permaily.com</a>.</li>
      <li>Open Settings, and choose Delete account. You may be asked to confirm it’s you.</li>
      <li>You get a message at your contact address when you ask, and if you cancel.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What happens, and when</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>At once,</b> your account closes and your connections end. Existing delivery stops within five seconds. Connections do not return if you cancel deletion. Every other device is signed out. During the seven-day cancellation window, you can still cancel, read and download your mail, or disconnect a mailbox.</li>
      <li><b>After the seven-day cancellation window,</b> removal is scheduled unless you cancelled; cleanup retries if it fails, and the exceptions below remain. Your account’s record (contact address, name, passkeys, linked providers, recovery codes, settings), your sessions, the devices that signed in, your connections, your receipts, and the entries that find your account are removed, and the account can’t be brought back. Your address can then sign up again as a new account.</li>
      <li><b>Your mailboxes:</b> Removing your account, or disconnecting a mailbox, removes its key from the current record, stops new reads and stops new mail arriving at its address. Its stored mail and rule-decision records are then removed in background cleanup. The backup and in-progress-request limits below still apply.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What stays, and for how long</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Backups:</b> Our database keeps a rolling one-day backup, including wrapped keys from before a mailbox was disconnected. Disconnecting prevents new reads through the approved service; an old database copy alone cannot undo that because the service checks the newest integrity record. A request that already opened the key may finish within its deadline, at most one minute. This is not immediate cryptographic erasure: a saved wrapped key, the root secret and a saved copy of the encrypted mail could still be used to recover that mail outside those checks. Backups do not become inaccessible merely because the live record was removed. The backup is of the database, which holds sealed message details, search content and keys; the original messages and their attachments are stored separately, encrypted, and removed under their own retention rules, not kept in that backup.</li>
      <li><b>Your connections:</b> Deleting your account ends every connection at once, and a connection does not come back if you cancel the deletion. When the account is removed, after the seven-day cancellation window, we remove your connections with it: the list of your connections, each connection’s record and its devices’ token records. If a change to that list was under way at that moment, it can write your account’s identifier into our index of accounts with connections again; we remove that entry on the next successful hourly cleanup pass. Such a change can also store a copy of one of those records after they were removed; nothing reads it, and a last cleanup pass at least 30 minutes after the removal deletes it, with the list of the removed records’ identifiers we keep until then to find it. A copy stored the same way for a connection that had ended and was already removed during the seven-day window can stay; nothing reads it either. What stays is an integrity record of each removal, kept for ten years so a removed record can never be restored. It names the record (your account’s, a connection’s or a device’s identifier) and when it was removed, with a version number and a hash, and nothing else. The tokens’ lookup entries expire within 30 days and are refused from the removal on. Until the account is removed, an earlier closed part of the list can still hold the connections it held then: app web addresses, connection identifiers and dates, and hashes of decided authorization requests. Database backups can keep removed records for a further day.</li>
      <li><b>The admin log:</b> entries naming your account’s identifier, if an admin acted on it, for 400 days from each entry.</li>
      <li><b>Integrity records:</b> small records of each change to your account, your mailboxes and your connections, with a name that for your account includes its identifier, a version, a hash and the time, locked for ten years. What happens to them after that isn’t decided yet.</li>
      <li><b>Your list of mailboxes:</b> After account removal, its mailbox list remains as a closed record to prevent new mailboxes being added to that account. Normally it is empty apart from the account identifier and record state. If conflicting integrity records prevent a mailbox from being safely removed, the list can also retain that mailbox’s identifier, address hash and creation date. Such a mailbox cannot be opened through the service; its remaining mail expires under its existing retention.</li>
      <li><b>Addresses that bounced or complained:</b> AWS keeps them until we remove them; write to us if yours is one.</li>
      <li><b>Records the law makes us keep</b>, such as a government request to preserve them, for as long as it requires.</li>
      <li><b>What a connection already received</b> is in its hands: ask it to delete it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Remove just one thing</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A provider you signed in with</b> (Google, GitHub, Facebook or Microsoft): unlink it in Settings, under Sign-in, unless it’s your only way left to sign in. We delete the identifier it gave us. If you signed up with it, your contact address came from it and stays: change it in Settings. You can also remove Permaily in your account at the provider.</li>
      <li><b>A passkey or a device</b>: remove the passkey, or sign the device out, in Settings.</li>
      <li><b>Mail</b>: delete a message in the Inbox, or disconnect the mailbox in Accounts.</li>
      <li><b>A connection</b>: revoke it, or one of its devices, in Connections. Its receipts stay until your account is removed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>If you signed in with Facebook</h2>
    <p>Facebook told us its identifier for you, your name and, if you allowed it, an address. We keep only the identifier, to sign you in, and never take Facebook’s word for an address: if you signed up with Facebook, you confirmed your contact address with a code. To delete what we keep from Facebook, unlink it in Settings; to delete everything, delete your account as above. Removing Permaily in your Facebook settings stops future sign-ins with it, and doesn’t delete your Permaily account or its data.</p>

    <h2>If you can’t sign in</h2>
    <p>Write to <a class="link" href={`mailto:${OPERATOR.privacy}`}>{OPERATOR.privacy}</a> from your account’s contact address. We’ll ask for your account to be deleted, which starts the same seven days; you get a message, and can still cancel if you sign in. We can’t delete an account for anyone who can’t show it’s theirs.</p>
    <p>More in the <a class="link" href={LINKS.privacy}>privacy policy</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Legal>
